      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Amlodipine 20 mg is above the FDA-labeled maximum of 10 mg once daily for adults. Doses over 10 mg add little blood pressure benefit and raise the risk of peripheral edema, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia. Your patient's normal kidney and liver function and lack of allergies don't change that limit.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg PO daily.** This is the maximum dose. It may be what was intended, or a reasonable step if the patient is already on 5 mg.\n- **Amlodipine 5 mg PO daily.** This is the usual starting dose, and 2.5 mg is used for frail patients.\n- **A second agent.** If 10 mg isn't controlling BP, adding an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide is usually preferred to going higher on amlodipine.\n\nPlease confirm the dose with your supervising resident or attending. If 20 mg is intentional, for example in a specific protocol or a patient already on that dose, tell me and I'll enter it with a note documenting the rationale and who approved it. Otherwise, tell me which dose you want and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
